      "question": {
        ":question/id": "84fdae08-a32b-5b62-94ec-677abfe81363",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
        ":question/problem": "Calculate the definite integral $\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{4 x}{x^{2} + 1}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "We start by changing the variable:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u & =x^{2} + 1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =2 x \\\\ \\text{d}u & =2 x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\,\\text{d}u & =4 x\\,\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we create a table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=x^{2} + 1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 1 & 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable is\n\n$$\n\\int_{0}^{2}\\frac{1}{(x^{2} + 1)}\\cdot 4 x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve it and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\cdot 2\\,\\text{d}u & =2\\int_{1}^{5}\\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =2\\ln(u)|_{1}^{5} \\\\  & =2(\\ln(5)-\\ln(1)) \\\\  & =2 \\log{\\left(5 \\right)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/answer-fields": [
